{{/*
  Context7 CLI credential (github.com/upstash/context7, `npx ctx7@latest`).

  WHY A CREDENTIAL FILE AND NOT AN ENV VAR. ctx7 resolves auth in exactly two
  places (utils/api.ts, utils/auth.ts): $CONTEXT7_API_KEY first, then this
  file. It used to be the env var, exported from a hand-written
  ~/.config/fish/conf.d/99-local.fish that was deliberately unmanaged and so
  never reached a new machine — the key existed on this laptop and nowhere
  else. A credential file fixes that and is strictly wider: it works for
  anything that runs ctx7, not just descendants of a fish login shell.

  A `ctx7sk-` key is a valid access_token. getValidAccessToken() only
  refreshes when `expires_at` has passed, so a credential with neither
  `expires_at` nor `refresh_token` is used verbatim, forever — which is what
  an API key is. `npx ctx7@latest login` would overwrite this file with an
  OAuth token; re-apply to put the key back.

  A TEMPLATE, not a symlink into ~/.dotfiles: chezmoi runs in symlink mode
  here and that repo is PUBLIC. Templates are written rather than linked, so
  `private_` produces a real 0600 file and the key lives only in `pass`
  (AI/context7/main, pushed to the private password-store repo) — no
  ciphertext in the public history, unlike the age-blob route the dekho and
  mail keys take.

  THE DOUBLE GUARD IS LOAD-BEARING, and the second half is not paranoia:

    * `stat` — a phase-1 install has no store yet, so it renders nothing and
      this file simply does not exist until bin/secrets-restore clones the
      store and re-applies this path. Same shape as the mail configs.

    * the --pinentry-mode=error probe (copied from bin/gpg-unlock) — a bare
      `pass` call would be a PROMPT on a locked agent. chezmoi's apply may run
      in the background with no tty, where pinentry-auto falls through to its
      display check and draws a Qt dialog on whatever monitor the agent
      inherited from tty1 — the hang documented in fish/conf.d/15-gpg.fish,
      which cost an SSH session on the NUC. Worse, a failing template function
      aborts the ENTIRE apply, so one locked agent would stop every unrelated
      file. The probe never prompts: it asks "is the key already cached?", and
      a locked agent answers `locked` and leaves the existing file alone.

  `|| printf locked` rather than letting gpg's exit status escape: `output`
  fails the template on a non-zero exit, which is the abort this is avoiding.
*/ -}}
{{- $entry := joinPath .chezmoi.homeDir ".password-store/AI/context7/main.gpg" -}}
{{- $probe := printf "gpg --batch --quiet --no-tty --pinentry-mode=error --decrypt %s >/dev/null 2>&1 && printf cached || printf locked" (squote $entry) -}}
{{- if and (stat $entry) (eq "cached" (output "sh" "-c" $probe)) -}}
{
  "access_token": {{ pass "AI/context7/main" | quote }},
  "token_type": "Bearer"
}
{{ end -}}
